1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,667
On the left is a mission,

2
00:00:02,117 --> 00:00:05,695
and on the right is that same mission, being played.

3
00:00:06,595 --> 00:00:09,007
An hour ago, neither of them existed.

4
00:00:10,807 --> 00:00:13,966
In this lecture, you put an editor on your computer,

5
00:00:14,416 --> 00:00:17,311
you make a mission with one line, and you fly it.

6
00:00:18,211 --> 00:00:20,531
You don't write anything yet,

7
00:00:20,981 --> 00:00:23,996
because today is about having the desk ready.

8
00:00:25,196 --> 00:00:29,035
A mission is plain text, and plain text wants an editor.

9
00:00:29,935 --> 00:00:32,574
A word processor changes what you type:

10
00:00:33,024 --> 00:00:37,065
it curls your quote marks and it joins your hyphens,

11
00:00:37,515 --> 00:00:39,369
and the game can read neither.

12
00:00:41,169 --> 00:00:43,711
So we use VS Code, which is free.

13
00:00:44,611 --> 00:00:46,904
You download it, and you run the installer.

14
00:00:47,804 --> 00:00:50,541
Only one page of it matters, and it's this one.

15
00:00:50,991 --> 00:00:53,459
Tick both of these boxes, and leave the rest.

16
00:00:55,259 --> 00:00:57,909
Then there are four names to learn for this window:

17
00:00:58,359 --> 00:01:00,789
the strip of icons, the panel beside it,

18
00:01:01,239 --> 00:01:03,178
the big area where your files open,

19
00:01:03,628 --> 00:01:05,436
and the bar along the bottom.

20
00:01:06,636 --> 00:01:09,552
VS Code doesn't know what a mission file is,

21
00:01:10,002 --> 00:01:11,830
so one add-on teaches it.

22
00:01:12,730 --> 00:01:15,844
VS Code calls an add-on an extension,

23
00:01:16,294 --> 00:01:20,084
which isn't the same thing as the extension on a file's name.

24
00:01:21,884 --> 00:01:23,466
The add-on is one file.

25
00:01:24,366 --> 00:01:27,851
I download it, and then I hand it to VS Code here:

26
00:01:28,301 --> 00:01:32,043
the three dots, and then install from V S I X.

27
00:01:33,843 --> 00:01:36,111
One warning, though: don't search for it.

28
00:01:37,011 --> 00:01:38,269
It isn't in this list,

29
00:01:38,719 --> 00:01:41,713
and these others have nothing to do with our game.

30
00:01:42,913 --> 00:01:45,091
Now back to the window from last time.

31
00:01:45,991 --> 00:01:49,283
Check the line first: it should end in data, missions.

32
00:01:50,183 --> 00:01:52,202
First I ask what I can start from,

33
00:01:52,652 --> 00:01:54,799
and there are five templates.

34
00:01:55,699 --> 00:01:57,765
We want this one, A M D.

35
00:01:59,565 --> 00:02:01,474
Now the line that makes a mission.

36
00:02:02,374 --> 00:02:06,058
It's create, then the folder's name, with no spaces.

37
00:02:06,508 --> 00:02:08,661
Then hyphen T, and the template.

38
00:02:09,561 --> 00:02:12,009
Then two hyphens and the word title,

39
00:02:12,459 --> 00:02:15,511
and in quote marks, the mission's title.

40
00:02:16,411 --> 00:02:19,650
That's the line the server will show in its list of missions,

41
00:02:20,100 --> 00:02:23,194
the same list you chose Legendary Missions from.

42
00:02:24,994 --> 00:02:26,784
It stops to ask one question,

43
00:02:27,234 --> 00:02:29,774
and pressing Enter means yes.

44
00:02:30,674 --> 00:02:34,263
And there's the folder, right beside the game's own missions.

45
00:02:35,463 --> 00:02:38,193
There's one more line, and you type it once.

46
00:02:39,093 --> 00:02:42,368
Missions share program parts called libraries.

47
00:02:43,268 --> 00:02:44,847
They came with the game,

48
00:02:45,297 --> 00:02:47,722
and create kept them just as they are.

49
00:02:48,622 --> 00:02:51,218
But they're mended and published again from time to time,

50
00:02:51,668 --> 00:02:54,459
so this fetches today's build of each one.

51
00:02:56,259 --> 00:02:59,658
Read the last line: my mission itself was not changed.

52
00:03:00,558 --> 00:03:04,589
And never leave off the end of this command, update libs,

53
00:03:05,039 --> 00:03:08,012
because without it, fetch does a different job.

54
00:03:09,212 --> 00:03:11,103
Now I open the mission in the editor,

55
00:03:11,553 --> 00:03:13,763
and I open the folder, not a file.

56
00:03:14,663 --> 00:03:16,936
So it's file, open folder,

57
00:03:17,386 --> 00:03:20,036
one click on my mission, and select folder.

58
00:03:21,836 --> 00:03:24,560
VS Code now asks whether I trust this folder.

59
00:03:25,460 --> 00:03:26,829
I wrote it, so yes.

60
00:03:27,729 --> 00:03:29,550
That matters more than it looks,

61
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:34,169
because until you say yes, the add-on only colors the file,

62
00:03:34,619 --> 00:03:36,754
and its checking has to wait.

63
00:03:38,554 --> 00:03:39,802
There are seven files here.

64
00:03:40,702 --> 00:03:43,420
This one is yours: mission.amd.

65
00:03:44,320 --> 00:03:48,199
This one, you'll change a few lines of, later on.

66
00:03:48,649 --> 00:03:50,553
And the other five are plumbing.

67
00:03:51,753 --> 00:03:54,998
There are three signs that the add-on is alive.

68
00:03:55,898 --> 00:03:59,370
There's color, and there are the letters A M D down here,

69
00:03:59,820 --> 00:04:02,942
and there's the outline: eight headings, one inside another.

70
00:04:04,742 --> 00:04:06,258
Now I'll break something on purpose.

71
00:04:07,157 --> 00:04:09,853
Line forty-seven is the heading of a scan record:

72
00:04:10,303 --> 00:04:13,458
the words Science shows when the crew scans the hulk.

73
00:04:14,358 --> 00:04:16,881
Here they are in the game, on the Science console,

74
00:04:17,331 --> 00:04:19,118
on the hulk's scan tab.

75
00:04:20,018 --> 00:04:23,328
Back in the file, I take off the part in round brackets,

76
00:04:23,778 --> 00:04:26,153
and a red wavy line appears.

77
00:04:27,053 --> 00:04:29,996
It's telling me the heading has lost its key.

78
00:04:30,896 --> 00:04:32,824
Control Z, and it's mended.

79
00:04:33,274 --> 00:04:34,896
And nobody typed a command.

80
00:04:36,696 --> 00:04:38,618
There are two keys to keep:

81
00:04:39,068 --> 00:04:42,170
control Z takes back, and control S saves.

82
00:04:43,070 --> 00:04:46,188
A dot on the tab means not saved,

83
00:04:46,638 --> 00:04:49,432
and the game reads the saved file, not my screen.

84
00:04:50,632 --> 00:04:54,476
Here's the same check, on the whole mission, from the command prompt.

85
00:04:55,376 --> 00:04:58,007
There's one word to look for, and it's clean.

86
00:04:58,907 --> 00:05:01,609
This command has a lecture all to itself.

87
00:05:02,809 --> 00:05:04,280
Now for the game itself.

88
00:05:05,180 --> 00:05:07,607
A run line starts several copies at once:

89
00:05:08,057 --> 00:05:11,355
a server, which runs the mission, and the consoles I ask for.

90
00:05:12,255 --> 00:05:15,449
I always look first, before I run anything.

91
00:05:15,899 --> 00:05:18,896
Dry run prints what it would start, and starts nothing.

92
00:05:20,696 --> 00:05:25,517
So it's server, helm, science, with commas and no spaces.

93
00:05:26,417 --> 00:05:28,888
Then hyphen M and my mission,

94
00:05:29,338 --> 00:05:34,423
and map equals nought, which means start the first map without asking me.

95
00:05:35,323 --> 00:05:37,471
Good. Now I run it for real.

96
00:05:39,271 --> 00:05:42,371
Three windows open, and each one says what it is.

97
00:05:43,271 --> 00:05:46,088
Here I am at Helm, in the window called helm.

98
00:05:46,538 --> 00:05:51,496
There's the station, and there, nine thousand beyond it, is an unknown hulk.

99
00:05:52,396 --> 00:05:55,715
I fly to it, and that's the first step of the sample quest.

100
00:05:56,615 --> 00:05:59,308
Here it is in the Quest Log, on the handheld:

101
00:05:59,758 --> 00:06:03,340
Find the Derelict is done, and Study the Derelict has appeared.

102
00:06:04,240 --> 00:06:05,882
And this is the Science window,

103
00:06:06,332 --> 00:06:08,705
with the hulk in its list of contacts.

104
00:06:10,505 --> 00:06:12,512
When I'm done, I close all three.

105
00:06:12,962 --> 00:06:15,586
Every one of them, before I start again.

106
00:06:16,786 --> 00:06:19,548
There's a second way, with no game windows at all.

107
00:06:20,448 --> 00:06:24,176
The tool plays the mission itself, and shows it in my browser.

108
00:06:25,976 --> 00:06:29,369
It prints about twenty lines, and three of them read like trouble.

109
00:06:30,269 --> 00:06:32,697
They aren't. They're printed for every new mission.

110
00:06:33,597 --> 00:06:37,145
Then it goes quiet, and the prompt doesn't come back.

111
00:06:38,045 --> 00:06:39,754
That's right, because it's running.

112
00:06:41,554 --> 00:06:43,414
So I open this address.

113
00:06:44,314 --> 00:06:46,985
This is a drawing of a console, and not the game.

114
00:06:47,435 --> 00:06:49,389
It's quick, and it's enough for a look.

115
00:06:50,289 --> 00:06:52,898
To stop it, press control C, here.

116
00:06:53,798 --> 00:06:56,498
And when a lecture says play it, use the game.

117
00:06:57,698 --> 00:07:00,416
Here are four slips, so that you've seen them.

118
00:07:01,316 --> 00:07:03,301
The first is create, twice.

119
00:07:03,751 --> 00:07:07,122
It refuses, and that's the tool protecting your work.

120
00:07:08,022 --> 00:07:11,447
The second is the mission's name where the consoles go:

121
00:07:11,897 --> 00:07:16,684
you get one window and no server, with only this note to tell you.

122
00:07:17,584 --> 00:07:19,970
The third is a space after a comma,

123
00:07:20,420 --> 00:07:24,315
which gives you a window with no name, and no note at all.

124
00:07:26,115 --> 00:07:27,480
And the fourth you've already met:

125
00:07:27,930 --> 00:07:31,657
an untrusted folder, and a file that's colored but not checked.

126
00:07:32,557 --> 00:07:36,363
The page has tables of more than thirty, and each one was tried.

127
00:07:37,563 --> 00:07:41,341
Now make a second mission, called Practice, without the page.

128
00:07:42,241 --> 00:07:45,174
Open it in VS Code, trust it,

129
00:07:45,624 --> 00:07:47,692
break line forty-seven and mend it,

130
00:07:48,142 --> 00:07:50,223
and then fly it with one console.

131
00:07:52,023 --> 00:07:56,235
After that, open My Mission again, and leave it open.

132
00:07:57,135 --> 00:08:00,957
Next time, you'll learn the marks that file is written in.
